ing that was really  quite important she didn't tell us kids  this at the time but she told this to  the older relatives  she was a very devout catholic as a  matter of fact she even taught ccd  as i i got to see when i went with her  to classes  and um you know like a lot of people  who are you could say  you know catholics and especially like  uh  catholics who have particular devotions  to  saints or live within certain  communities they often have a lot of  interesting ideas about you know what  god would allow you to do  or what you know what sort of  intercessions you might might work for  and  and so she said this she said  if i make it to heaven and i'm allowed  to do this  whenever the family is in trouble  i will send you monarch butterflies to  let you know that  everything is going to be okay and to  fortify you  in doing the sorts of things that that  you ought to do  to you know help you feel better to give  you a sign that  you're not alone all these sorts of  things  and nobody really took much notice of  that  at the time now monarch butterflies  as i'll talk about in a bit they're  really not uncommon at certain times of  the years in the midwest because we're  on a major  migration um you know  whatever you call it the path where they  they go and they  live on a lot of the native and  cultivated  um plants in in the areas we'll talk  about in a minute  but this story this this promise  it came to fruition when my dad died  so my dad dies in in august  august 2nd of 1982  at only 36 years old you know lingering  illness that takes him all the way up to  the mayo clinic  even they couldn't figure out what was  going on and he eventually  he eventually dies so  during the funeral which was  you know near well it would have been  genesee depot where saint paul's church  was and the saint paul  churchyard near the border of wales and  delafield  where where we lived where we had our  house where i grew up  um i remember all of us being there  at the the cemetery which i still go and  visit occasionally because both my  my dad and my mom and my grandpa and  grandma's ashes are buried there  and you know it was a typical sort of  thing you've got the the casket that's  being lowered into the ground  and all these people dressed in  uncomfortably hot clothing right because  it was suits and ties at the time  and fancy dresses and things like that  for funerals  even in the middle of an august summer  heat wave  and now i didn't see this but my mom  told this story later on she said as  the casket was being lowered into the  ground  you know and it's being lowered quite  slowly she saw a single monarch  butterfly  you know flit its way over land on the  casket  and she like she was you know thinking  well  maybe i'm i'm stricken with grief and  i'm hallucinating or something like that  so she bumps her friend uh standing next  to her and she's like do you see that  and her friend's like see what  you see that monarch butterfly on the  casket her friend says  yes so what there's a butterfly and  the butterfly went all the way down into  the grave  with the casket as it was being lowered  and my mother interpreted that  as a sign that my grandmother  ann sadler was sending you know  a sign to to not be worried and it was a  time when we were  very worried i mean my dad was the bread  earner  and um you know we were supposed to go  down to houston  uh where we were you know before he got  sick we were getting set to move  he'd recently uh had us purchase this  you know this boat  which we had to sell to one of our  neighbors on the cheap  and you know my family was going from  the  middle class that they'd clawed their  way into over the years back down into  the working class my mom had to go to  work within months of my  my dad dying and and there were  questions about you know  what all this was going to mean for us  so this is you know a significant time  and you know think about my mom  36 years old and  her she becomes a widow with with two  young children um so that certainly  would be a time  worth worrying about and so  you know however you want to interpret  this my mom took that  as a sign from grandma sadler that  things were going to be okay  and my mom took the monarch  as sort of her symbol just like her  mother-in-law  anne sadler had and she you know she had  brooches and she had jewelry of other  sorts and  things made for her to kind of  commemorate that  and you know it's um  it's something that has sort of  proliferated  into my generation i don't know how my  sister feels about it exactly but i know  for myself that there have been many  times  where things were going really look like  they were going south for me  in many respects i've had kind of a  tumultuous life  you know often lurching from one crisis  to the next  and quite often i will  wind up even out of season seeing a  monarch  doing something what should i make of  this is it grandma sadler is it my mom  you know asking for permission to  somehow  you know do weird things in the  background  who knows you know i mean i'm not a  superstitious kind of person  um but maybe there's maybe there's  something to this you never you never  really know and i  i it's a wonderful set of memories that  i have it  every time that i do see a monarch  in these sort of circumstances i'm  reminded of my mom and i'm reminded of  my  grandma you know ann sadler and monique  sadler two  really wonderful people to to people  that  made other people's lives so much better  in  so many respects and um  you know really made a huge impact on on  me  and i want to talk just a little bit  about monarchs before i end this  so the monarch butterfly again if you  haven't seen  one you know google it they're beautiful  they're these  dark orange butterflies with you know  black  ribs in their wings and they live  on things like um you know in the carrot  family  queens and lace and if you have a  carrots in your garden  monarch caterpillars which are this  beautiful  you know black and white and yellow  striped things will come on them and  land  and and eat them up there's other things  that they like as well  milkweed which makes them poisonous  that's why  birds don't don't mess with them there's  even a butterfly that isn't the monarch  but  pretends to be the monarch so that birds  will leave it alone as well  and you know right now  it's unusual even to see one later on in  the summer  we'll see lots and lots of them and by  you know by by the fall they're already  starting to migrate southward  and you know this is kind of a cool  story there's there's an area in mexico  where the monarchs the all the ones that  are still alive because many of them die  you know on the way they will fly all  the way from here or even as  far north as parts of canada down to  mexico  and congregate in this very small  area in which there'll be you know i  don't know  millions of these beautiful butterflies  and then you know as winter ends they  will make their way  back north lay their eggs the  caterpillars will hatch  they'll eat they'll turn into these uh  again you know very  stunning green chrysalises and then  then the monarch butterflies themselves  will come out and so  i've you know even before my grandma  sadler died  i i was always when i'd see monarch  butterflies i thought they're the most  beautiful of the  butterflies that we had here in in  wisconsin  because it was mostly monarchs and then  a few other things and moths and then  our sulphur butterflies which are also  pretty but they're not monarch pretty so  um  you know that's a little bit about my  family's  connection attachment  you know weird beliefs about the monarch  butterflies they're they're also  important in other people's heritage  as well but um you know  i think in different ways so  perhaps you find it interesting perhaps  you find it uh  kind of silly or something like that but  i have this this very strong attachment  it gives me much  joy to see them so i was very happy  today to see  the first monarch of the season  you
 

